The three-dimensional radial vector is \[\vec{r}=x\hat{i}+y\hat{j}+z\hat{k}\] Its divergence is \[\nabla \cdot \vec{r}
=
\frac{\partial x}{\partial x}
+
\frac{\partial y}{\partial y}
+
\frac{\partial z}{\partial z}\] \[=1+1+1\] \[=3\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) 3}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For \(\vec{r}=x\hat{i}+y\hat{j}+z\hat{k}\), \[\nabla \cdot \vec{r}=3\]</li><li>In \(n\)-dimensions, divergence of the position vector equals \(n\).</li></ul></div>

The given signal is \[x(t)=8\sin\left(0.8\pi t+\frac{\pi}{4}\right).\] Comparing with the standard form \[x(t)=A\sin(\omega t+\phi),\] the angular frequency is \[\omega=0.8\pi\;\text{rad/s}.\] The time period is \[T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega}
=\frac{2\pi}{0.8\pi}
=\frac{2}{0.8}
=2.5\,\text{s}.\] The amplitude and phase shift do not affect the period. \[\boxed{\text{(d) }2.5\,\text{s}}\]

Given, \[x[n]=\{1,-1\},\] and \[y[n]=\{1,0,0,0,-1\}.\] Since \[y[n]=x[n]*h[n],\] we determine the impulse response \(h[n]\). Using the convolution with \[x[n]=\delta[n]-\delta[n-1],\] we get \[y[n]=h[n]-h[n-1].\] Now compute the samples sequentially: \[y[0]=h[0]=1\] \[y[1]=h[1]-h[0]=0
\;\Rightarrow\;
h[1]=1\] \[y[2]=h[2]-h[1]=0
\;\Rightarrow\;
h[2]=1\] \[y[3]=h[3]-h[2]=0
\;\Rightarrow\;
h[3]=1.\] Finally, \[y[4]=-h[3]=-1,\] which is satisfied. Hence, \[h[n]=\{1,1,1,1\}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) }h[n]=\{1,1,1,1\}}\]

Given, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 &amp; 2\\
0 &amp; 2
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
B=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}\] The characteristic equation is \[|sI-A|=0\] \[\left|
\begin{matrix}
s+1 &amp; -2\\
0 &amp; s-2
\end{matrix}
\right|=0\] \[(s+1)(s-2)=0\] \[s=-1,\qquad s=2\] Since one eigenvalue lies in the right-half plane, the system is unstable. The controllability matrix is \[Q_c=
\begin{bmatrix}
B &amp; AB
\end{bmatrix}\] \[AB=
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 &amp; 2\\
0 &amp; 2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
2
\end{bmatrix}\] \[Q_c=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 2\\
1 &amp; 2
\end{bmatrix}\] \[|Q_c|=-2\neq 0\] Hence, the system is completely controllable. \[\boxed{(C)\;\text{Unstable but controllable}}\]

