Assume \[\overline{V}_{p}=V_{p}\angle 0^\circ\] For the ideal transformer, \[\overline{V}_{s}=2\overline{V}_{p}
=2V_{p}\angle 0^\circ\] Since the load is purely resistive, \[\overline{I}_{L}=1\angle 0^\circ\,\text{A}\] Using the transformer current ratio, \[\overline{I}_{p}'=2\overline{I}_{L}
=2\angle 0^\circ\,\text{A}\] The magnetizing current lags the applied voltage by \(90^\circ\), \[\overline{I}_{\mu}
=
1\angle(-90^\circ)\,\text{A}\] Therefore, the total primary current is \[\overline{I}_{p}
=
\overline{I}_{p}'
+
\overline{I}_{\mu}\] \[\overline{I}_{p}
=
2\angle 0^\circ
+
1\angle(-90^\circ)\] \[\overline{I}_{p}
=
(2-j1)\,\text{A}\] \[|\overline{I}_{p}|
=
\sqrt{2^2+1^2}
=
2.236\,\text{A}\] \[\angle \overline{I}_{p}
=
-\tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)
=
-26.56^\circ\] \[\overline{I}_{p}
=
2.236\angle(-26.56^\circ)\,\text{A}\] Hence, the correct option is \[\boxed{\text{C}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Magnetizing current always lags the applied voltage by approximately \(90^\circ\).</li><li>Total primary current is the phasor sum of reflected load current and magnetizing current.</li></ul></div>

For separately excited machine, at no-load, \[P=0,\qquad I_{a}=0\] Hence, \[V_{t}=E_{a}+I_{a}R_{a}=E_{a}\] Given induced emf is \(200\,\text{V}\) at \(1500\,\text{rpm}\). \[E=K_{m}\phi\omega\] \[K_{m}\phi=\frac{E}{\omega}\] \[K_{m}\phi=
\frac{200}{1500\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{60}\right)}
=1.273\] Since field voltage is unchanged, \[K_{m}\phi=\text{constant}\] Torque equation, \[T=(K_{m}\phi)I_{a}\] \[5=1.273I_{a}\] \[I_{a}=3.927\,\text{A}\] Under loaded condition, \[N=1400\,\text{rpm}\] Back emf, \[E_{a}=1.273\times1400\times\frac{2\pi}{60}\] \[E_{a}=186.63\,\text{V}\] Armature resistance, \[R_{a}=\frac{V_{t}-E_{a}}{I_{a}}\] \[R_{a}=\frac{200-186.63}{3.927}\] \[R_{a}=3.4\,\Omega\] \[\boxed{B}\]

